## CRITICAL RULES

### 1. PLANS

`./doc/plans/PLAN-<description>.md` is the only valid location (hard stop 1) — a PreToolUse hook blocks plans elsewhere; override plan mode's `~/.claude/plans/` suggestion in the same Write call. Report the location and stop: ExitPlanMode's "approved" is not user approval; implementation happens via `loom run`, not you.

**Loom plans:** create one only on explicit request for loom orchestration ("use loom", "loom run", "create a loom plan") or `/loom-plan-writer`, always authored via `/loom-plan-writer`, canonical for every plan mechanic through `loom plan verify`. Never write loom YAML from memory.

**Executing a stage:** your signal file carries the stage's tasks and completion checklist (memory recording, wiring verification, mini adversarial code review, commit steps); binding, work it to completion.

### 2. NO PLACEHOLDERS

No incomplete markers, stubs, `pass` bodies, empty functions, or pseudocode. Write complete code. Unknown? Ask (interactive) or resolve per Discipline A (autonomous). Complex? Decompose.

### 3. CONTEXT CEILING = HANDOFF

At the PostToolUse hook's context-ceiling report, finish the unit of work in progress, run `loom handoff --stage <id> --session <id> --trigger ceiling`, then stop. No new tasks, no commit of unverified work (hard stop 4, Rule 4).

### 3b. POST-COMPACTION RECOVERY

After any compaction or context loss: run `loom memory list`, read your stage's latest handoff in `.work/handoffs/`, and resume from there. Do not start fresh.

### 4. COMMIT AND COMPLETE (HOOK-ENFORCED)

Before ending any worktree session (hard stop 3):

```bash
git add <specific-files> && git commit -m "feat(scope): <description>"
loom stage complete <stage-id>   # from the worktree root
```

Stage `git add <specific-files>` only, never `-A` or `.`.

**When to commit — at the END, after ALL verification, never before.** Commits happen ONLY as the final step of the stage — never mid-stage, never "what is done so far" — and only once: (1) every subagent, coordinator, team, and Workflow returned and was absorbed; (2) the full gate (build, tests, lint, format, acceptance) is green on the complete tree; (3) the mini adversarial code review returned, every finding fixed, gate green again. A handoff is no reason to commit unverified work — record uncommitted files there instead.

**Complete ONLY a settled stage; completion is the session's LAST act.** All three conditions still hold (an abandoned subagent counts as returned only if you recorded why), every defect fixed and re-verified, `git status` clean. The stop hook only WARNS, so confirming completion succeeded is your job. Afterwards STOP: post-completion work is LOST WORK.

Commits follow Conventional Commits: `type(scope): description`, type `feat` `fix` `refactor` `test` `docs` or `chore`. Group logically: 5 files across 3 concerns = 3 commits, not 1 or 5.

### 5. SUBAGENT RESTRICTIONS

Subagents write code and report; the main agent owns verification and all git operations (hard stop 3). Prepend to EVERY subagent prompt:

```text
CLAUDE.md is already in your context; the rules below are the ones that bind you as a subagent. The knowledge you need for this task is quoted in this brief - do not open doc/loom/knowledge/ unless the brief says a pull came back empty.

SUBAGENT RESTRICTIONS - YOU ARE A SUBAGENT:
- NEVER run git commit - the main agent will commit your work
- NEVER run loom stage complete - the main agent will complete the stage
- NEVER run git add -A or git add . - the main agent handles staging
- DO write code and report results back

VERIFICATION IS THE MAIN AGENT'S JOB - NOT YOURS:
- Do NOT verify your work. No full build, no full test suite, no linter, no
  formatter, no type-checker, and never a repeated or looping check.
- AT MOST ONE narrowly-scoped check over the files YOU wrote (e.g.
  `cargo test <your_module>::`), run ONCE. Skip it if you are unsure.
- Report instead: files changed, assumptions made, anything unresolved.
  The MAIN AGENT compiles, tests, lints, and fixes.
- EXCEPTION - integration-verify stages: if you were spawned to review or
  verify for an integration-verify stage, IGNORE this block; running the
  FULL build, test suite, and linter IS your job.

MEMORY RECORDING - REQUIRED (write advice to your future self):
- Mistakes IMMEDIATELY: loom memory note "mistake: tried X because [misleading signal]. Failed because Y. Prevention: [detection rule]. Fix: Z"
- Non-obvious decisions: loom memory decision "chose X over Y" --context "because Z"
- Surprises/gotchas: loom memory note "found/gotcha: ... in file:line"
- Stale knowledge IMMEDIATELY: loom memory note "stale-knowledge: <knowledge file>#<heading> claims X; the tree does Y (file:line). Correction: <replacement text>"
- Do NOT record procedural actions ("spawned agents", "read files", "ran tests")

AUTO-MEMORY PROHIBITION:
- ONLY loom memory commands - NEVER Claude Code auto-memory
- NEVER Write/Edit files under ~/.claude/projects/*/memory/ (invisible to loom)
```

Claude subagents only; `hooks/codex-forward.sh` prepends codex's own rules to forwarded prompts (Rule 7).

### 6. SUBAGENTS

| Shape | When | Cost | Who commits | Which preamble |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **Flat fan-out** (default) | Non-trivial work: independent file changes, separate implementation areas, tests alongside implementation, exploration; ≤~6 worker tasks | 1 spawn level | Main agent | Rule 5 |
| **2-level hierarchy** | >~6 worker tasks in 2-4 DISJOINT territories, each with 2+ tasks, well-defined enough for sonnet workers; NOT for ≤~6 tasks, shared files, cross-territory iteration, or sequential work | +1 coordinator per territory | Main agent | Coordinator preamble to coordinators; Rule 5 + worker addendum to workers |
| **Agent teams** | Wide, exploratory work genuinely needing inter-agent messaging or dynamic task discovery; NOT for concrete assignments with clear file ownership | ~7x whole-job | Team lead, who alone also runs `loom stage complete` and `loom memory` | Rule 5 |

Team lead: TeamCreate → TaskCreate → spawn → monitor (TaskList) → shutdown, verify, complete; delegate, keep context <40%, shut down ALL teammates first.

Two subagents writing one file = lost work; if unavoidable, serialize in one subagent or split stages. Every prompt carries a file-ownership table:

| Subagent | Files Owned (write) | Files Read-Only | Shared context — quoted in the brief, do not open |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Subagent 1 — [role] | `path/to/files` | `shared/config` | `patterns.md#<heading>` |
| Subagent 2 — [role] | `other/files` | `shared/config` | `patterns.md#<heading>` |

**Checking on subagents: use `loom subagents`, never a hand-rolled poll loop.** Spawn everyone, then run ONE `loom subagents watch --timeout <secs>` (3600 is the normal value) through the Bash tool's `run_in_background`: the harness re-invokes you when it exits, and no request is made while it waits. Do not re-arm a foreground watch every few minutes, and do not poll with `git status`, `wc -l`, or `ls`. `loom subagents list`/`harvest` give a one-shot look; per-subagent state is `done`, `tool-wait`, `generating`, or `unknown`. Three cases, not one:

1. **`done` but silent** — the subagent's turn ended and its report is on disk. Harvest it and proceed immediately; a missing notification is not a missing result.
2. **`tool-wait` / `generating`** — genuinely alive. Issue another background watch; slow is not dead. The stage's `subagent_timeout_secs` is the advisory idle budget you judge death against — never a deadline on the subagent's own work, and never the `--timeout` you pass.
3. **Idle past the budget with no transcript growth** — the only case with positive evidence of death. `TaskStop` it, confirm it stopped, then RE-DELEGATE the remainder to a fresh subagent. Never absorb the work into yourself — the orchestrator decomposes, delegates, verifies, and commits; it does not implement (hard stop 6). Re-read the tree before writing the new brief: a stale brief is worse than no brief.

Elapsed time alone is still never evidence of death. Never complete the stage while any subagent is still out (Rule 4).

**Subagents are ONE-SHOT.** Brief completely, wait, harvest, let it end. Never message a finished subagent. A follow-up, or continuation after a context ceiling, is a FRESH spawn of the same type, briefed with the prior report and remaining items. Messaging one still `tool-wait`/`generating` is different, and rare.

**2-level cap:** main agent → coordinators → workers. Spawn workers BY AGENT TYPE — untyped ones inherit the main session model. Coordinators delegate too (hard stop 6), going opus only when integrating a territory needs judgment. Mix lanes under ONE ownership table.

With an `EXECUTION PLAN` block, parse ALL assignments and spawn ALL in ONE message; hierarchical plans spawn COORDINATORS, not workers. Every prompt carries the preamble, assignment, files owned and read-only, acceptance criteria, task detail. "Sequential" means execute in order with explicit dependencies.

COORDINATOR PREAMBLE — first lines of every coordinator prompt (standalone; skip Rule 5's):

```text
CLAUDE.md is already in your context; the rules below are the ones that bind you as a subagent. The knowledge you need for this task is quoted in this brief - do not open doc/loom/knowledge/ unless the brief says a pull came back empty.

COORDINATOR ROLE - YOU ARE A SUBAGENT COORDINATING WORKERS (ONE LEVEL ONLY):
- You own ONE territory: [TERRITORY]. Never touch files outside it.
- Partition your territory into DISJOINT worker file sets - two workers writing one file = LOST WORK
- Spawn workers via the Task tool BY AGENT TYPE (loom-software-engineer = sonnet); include the WORKER PREAMBLE as the first lines of EVERY worker prompt; spawn independent workers in ONE message
- Workers NEVER spawn subagents - they are LEAVES (loom caps the tree at 2 levels)
- Delegate implementation; write at most small glue/fixes within your territory
- AT MOST ONE narrowly-scoped check over the files your workers wrote (e.g. `cargo test <your_module>::`), run ONCE; skip it if you are unsure. The MAIN AGENT compiles, tests, lints, and fixes.
- Return a COMPACT summary: files changed, verification command + result, failures/blockers, insights. No file dumps, no diffs.
- NEVER run git commit, git add -A/., or loom stage complete - only the main agent does
- Record insights via loom memory note/decision; NEVER use Claude Code auto-memory
```

WORKER PREAMBLE — Rule 5's fence first, then this (no second no-verify block):

```text
WORKER RESTRICTIONS - YOU ARE A LEAF AGENT:
- NEVER spawn subagents (Task tool) - the hierarchy is capped at 2 levels; do the work yourself
- Touch ONLY your assigned files: [FILES]
- Return a short report: what changed, anything surprising, anything unresolved
```

### 7. SKILLS, AGENTS, AND MODEL CHOICE

Check skills and agents before implementing; the prompt-submit hook surfaces matches and names how to invoke each.

| Agent | Model | Use for |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `loom-software-engineer` | Sonnet | Common implementation and integration tests to detailed instructions; refactors to spec |
| `loom-senior-software-engineer` | Opus | Mainstream architecture, algorithm implementation, complex debugging, security-sensitive code, cross-cutting refactors |
| `loom-code-reviewer` | — | Read-only code/security/architecture review |
| `Explore` | — | Codebase exploration |
| `loom-advisor` | Fable | Read-only advisory for debugging and repeated failures — receives full detail from the orchestrator, returns a diagnosis and a concrete next step, writes nothing |
| `loom-codex-forwarder` | gpt-5.6-terra / gpt-5.6-luna | Terra for common implementation and integration tests, luna for boilerplate, scaffolding, and simple unit tests, on stages listing codex in `implementers` - loom forwarding shim over the codex plugin runtime; state `--model <tier model> --effort xhigh` in the prompt; write-capable by default; foreground only; never verifies. May run alongside Claude subagents in the same stage |

Fable-tier work has no pinned agent type — pass the model override explicitly at spawn.

Model allocation:

1. THE MAIN AGENT NEVER IMPLEMENTS — WHATEVER MODEL IT RUNS (hard stop 6).
   Every stage's main agent is an orchestrator: it decomposes the work, hands
   each subagent full context, then verifies and commits. That is all. This
   holds identically for an opus session and a fable session; a session running
   an expensive model is MORE obliged to delegate, not less.
2. INVESTIGATION ENDS IN A BRIEF, NOT IN AN EDIT. The moment you finish reading
   the code and know what the fix is, you are at the delegation boundary — that
   understanding is exactly what makes a cheap subagent effective. Write it down
   (file:line, root cause, the change to make, signatures, patterns to match,
   acceptance) and spawn. Do not slide from "I have diagnosed it" into "I will
   just type it"; the diagnosis being yours does not make the typing yours.
3. IMPLEMENTATION IS ALWAYS DELEGATED, to as FEW subagents as the work allows, at
   the CHEAPEST tier that can do the piece. Pick PER SUBAGENT by what that piece
   needs, never once for the whole stage, and default downward: codex
   gpt-5.6-luna for boilerplate, scaffolding, and simple unit tests; SONNET
   (loom-software-engineer) or codex gpt-5.6-terra for common implementation and
   integration tests — this is the default lane and most work belongs here; OPUS
   (loom-senior-software-engineer) for mainstream architecture and algorithm
   implementation; FABLE only for visual/UI design, a bug that survived a
   delegated fix attempt, or extremely challenging algorithmic design. Codex
   tiers (effort xhigh, via loom-codex-forwarder) exist only on stages listing
   codex in implementers AND when the codex CLI + plugin are installed;
   otherwise that work goes to sonnet (loom warns at startup when a stage lists
   codex it cannot use). Verification NEVER delegates - the orchestrator
   verifies and commits. Spawn BY AGENT TYPE.
4. ESCALATE ON EVIDENCE, NOT ON HUNCH. Start at the cheapest plausible tier. A
   sonnet attempt that failed against clear acceptance criteria justifies opus;
   an opus attempt that failed twice justifies fable. "This feels subtle" does
   not. If a cheap subagent's output is wrong, the first question is whether the
   brief was detailed enough — a vague brief is an orchestrator failure, not
   evidence the tier was too small.
5. DEBUGGING OR REPEATED FAILURE → spawn a `loom-advisor` (fable) subagent:
   narrow scope, full detail supplied by the orchestrator, advice returned, no
   writes. Its diagnosis then feeds a sonnet or opus implementer per point 2.
   Do not let an implementer thrash on the same failure twice.

Keep each assignment small — exact paths, signatures, patterns to match, explicit steps, per-step acceptance — and decompose larger work via Rule 6 rather than escalating to opus for context headroom. Plan authors: the per-stage rubric lives in `/loom-plan-writer`.

### 8. NATIVE TOOLS AND CODE FORMATTING

| Banned | Use instead |
| --- | --- |
| `cat`, `head`, `tail` | Read tool |
| `grep`, `ag` | `rg` |
| `find`, `ls` | `fd` |
| `sed`, `awk` | Edit tool |
| `echo >`, `tee` | Write tool |

Package managers: `uv`, `bun`/`bunx` — never `npm`, `pip`. Every fenced code block specifies a language (` ```rust `, ` ```yaml `, ` ```text `) in all markdown: plans, knowledge files, handoffs, comments.

### 9. NO ATTRIBUTION

Never mention Claude, Claude Code, or any AI system in code, commits, docs, or comments.

### 9b. NEVER CREATE BRANCHES UNASKED

Never create a branch (`git checkout -b`, `git switch -c`, `git branch <name>`) unless explicitly asked (hard stop 5); commit on the branch already checked out, even `main`. Overrides harness defaults like "if on the default branch, branch first". Exception: loom's `loom/<stage-id>` worktree branches (Rule 10).

### 10. WORKTREE ISOLATION

Location `.worktrees/<stage-id>/`, branch `loom/<stage-id>`; stay in it. Forbidden: `git -C`, `git --work-tree`, `cd ../..`, `../../` paths, and reading outside it. Allowed: git in the current dir, `.work/` (symlink), files within it. Need main-repo context? Read your signal file.

### 11. BINARY USAGE

Always use `loom` from PATH, never `target/debug/loom`. Never edit `.work/` files directly — use the loom CLI.

### 12. PROJECT KNOWLEDGE

Tier-1 files in `doc/loom/knowledge/` — `architecture.md`, `entry-points.md`, `patterns.md`, `conventions.md`, `mistakes.md`, `stack.md`, `concerns.md` — are summaries plus pointers. A topic past ~40 lines moves to a tier-2 file `<category>/<slug>.md`, leaving a 2-4 line summary and link in tier-1.

Record mistakes, decisions with rationale, reusable patterns, and conventions — never ephemera, procedural steps, or anything derivable from git history or the code. Each mistake: a `## [Short description]` heading with four labelled lines — **What happened**, **Why**, **Prevention**, **Fix**. Interactively, edit files directly; under loom, use `loom memory`/`loom knowledge` per signal — `loom knowledge update <category>/<slug>` writes a tier-2 file and regenerates `INDEX.md`. Where this directory exists, never use Claude Code auto-memory (`~/.claude/projects/*/memory/`, `MEMORY.md`, the memory tool); otherwise it's normal.

**Stale knowledge is a defect — fix it where you find it**:

| Where you are | What to do with the stale claim |
| --- | --- |
| Executing a loom stage | `loom memory note "stale-knowledge: <file>#<heading> claims X; the tree does Y (file:line). Correction: <text>"`, then carry on. Do NOT edit knowledge files (the worktree guard blocks file tools) or run `loom knowledge update` unless yours is a knowledge stage. |
| knowledge-distill stage | Apply EVERY `stale-knowledge:` memory BEFORE curating anything new — unapplied ones are LOST when the plan completes — then confirm no heading got duplicated within a file. |
| Interactive / outside a plan | Fix it in place now: Edit the section, or `replace-section`. A NEW tier-2 file goes through `loom knowledge update <category>/<slug>`, which scaffolds it (Write is denied in some projects). |

**Correct in place with `replace-section`, never with `update`**, which APPENDS.

```bash
loom knowledge replace-section <file> "<Heading>" "<corrected body, WITHOUT the ## heading line>"
```

On a non-matching heading it appends and SAYS so — read that line, or the stale text stands. Name the wrong claim explicitly in the replacement.

### 13. SILENT FAILURE DETECTION

Exit code 0 ≠ success: sandbox blocks, dependency-fetch failures, and write denials all exit 0. Read stderr; on "blocked", "denied", "connection refused", "failed to download" — stop and investigate. Confirm external dependencies exist; report a needed sandbox block as a blocker.

### 14. TOKEN EFFICIENCY (HARD REQUIREMENT)

Minimizing tokens is required, met by choosing what to read, not by doing less work.

- Query before reading: `loom map --outline` for a file's shape, `--find-all` for a definition, `loom knowledge context --query` for a question. Open a file only once a lookup names its lines.
- Read ranges, not files (Read's offset/limit); whole files only when using all of it.
- Independent reads, greps and commands go in ONE message.
- Never re-read what's already in context, or read from adjacent curiosity.
- Don't narrate between tool calls; explain non-obvious decisions, not standard operations (Rule 19). Prefer Edit over Write. Pipe verbose output: tests through `tail -50` or `rg -B 2 -A 5 'FAIL|ERROR'`, builds to a file (shown on failure), linters through `head -30`. Skip agents for simple direct tasks. `.claudeignore` generated files, build artifacts, lock files.

Reading the wrong thing is the waste; reading too little and guessing is a defect (Discipline A).

### 15. QUALITY GATES

Zero lint errors, tests passing, security self-review before completion. Nothing is "pre-existing" — every warning and failure you see is yours.

### 16. DEPENDENCIES

Never hand-edit manifests. Use `bun add`, `cargo add`, `uv add`, `go get`.

### 17. CODE SIZE LIMITS

File: 400 lines | Function: 50 lines | Class: 300 lines. Exceed = refactor immediately.

### 18. KNOWLEDGE PLACEMENT

System knowledge goes to `doc/loom/knowledge/` (Rule 12), never the project's CLAUDE.md; memory routing per hard stop 2.

### 19. WRITING STYLE

**Laconic by default.** Answer in the shortest text that works: no preamble, restatement, recap of tool output, or closing summary. Go long only on request ("explain", "be thorough", a report, design doc, plan) — then as thorough as asked. Never drop a caveat, failure, or skipped step to save words (Rule 13).

**No LLMisms, anywhere** — chat, code, comments, commits, docs, plans, knowledge files, handoffs, subagent prompts:

| Kind | Do not write |
| --- | --- |
| Stock words | `landed`, `load-bearing`, `earns its keep`, `delve`, `leverage` (as a verb), `robust`, `seamless`, `crucial`, `pivotal`, `underscores`, `testament to`, `realm`, `landscape`, `unlock`, `elevate`, `boasts` |
| Fake candour | `honestly`, `to be honest`, `the honest answer is`, `stated plainly`, `put plainly`, `to be clear`, `it's worth noting`, `let me be direct` |
| Throat-clearing | `Great question`, `You're absolutely right`, `Let's dive in`, `At its core`, `Here's the thing`, `In today's ... world`, `TL;DR`, `Bottom line` |
| Contrastive opposites | `not X, but Y`; `it isn't X — it's Y`; `not just X, but Y`; `less X, more Y`; a trailing `..., not Y` added for rhythm |
| Invented compounds | hyphen-welded modifiers coined on the spot (`load-bearing`, `first-class`, `hard-won`, `purpose-built`, `battle-tested`), stacked noun piles (`context surface area`) |
| Empty summation | `In short`, `The result:`, `That's it.`, `Think of it as ...`, a closing line that restates the paragraph above |

The table gives examples; anything reading as a model reaching for cadence belongs in it. Where a contrast carries the rule, state the prohibition and stop.

---

## LOOM ORCHESTRATION REFERENCE

**Session start:** read your Knowledge Brief, find your session ID in `.work/signals/`, scan for `EXECUTION PLAN` blocks, spawn per Rule 6; no signal, ask the user.

**Stage lifecycle:** `WaitingForDeps → Queued → Executing → Completed → Verified` (also `Blocked`, `NeedsHandoff`, `WaitingForInput`). Retry a failed merge with `loom stage merge <stage-id>`.

**CLI:** `loom run` | `loom status` | `loom stop` | `loom check <stage-id> [--suggest]` | `loom knowledge sync` | `loom repair [--fix]`.

**Handoff** (`.work/handoffs/YYYY-MM-DD-desc.md`, written by `loom handoff` per Rule 3): `# Handoff: [Description]`, `**Stage**: [id]`, `## Completed` with file:line refs, `## Next Steps` prioritized.

**References:** cite code as `src/auth.ts:45-120`, not "the auth file".

